description:
A checkout can apply one discount to an order. Write the interface, three implementations and a chooser:

```csharp
public interface IDiscount
{
    string Name { get; }
    decimal Apply(decimal total);       // the total after the discount, never below 0
}

public class PercentOff(decimal percent) : IDiscount        // Name "<percent>% off", e.g. "10% off"
public class AmountOff(decimal amount, decimal minimum) : IDiscount   // Name "<amount> off over <minimum>"; only when total >= minimum
public class FreeItem(decimal itemPrice) : IDiscount        // Name "free item"; the cheapest of nothing: just subtracts itemPrice

public static class Checkout
{
    public static (string Name, decimal Total) Best(decimal total, IEnumerable<IDiscount> discounts)
}
```

- All results are rounded to cents (halves away from zero); amounts in names use invariant formatting without trailing zeros (`10`, `2.5`).
- `Best` returns the discount giving the lowest total; on a tie, the one that comes first. With no discount that lowers the total, it returns `("none", total)`.

Then make orders sortable: a `record Order(string Id, int Priority, DateTime Placed) : IComparable<Order>` that sorts by `Priority` (1 first), then by `Placed` (earlier first), then by `Id`.

